{{define "content"}}
<vox-hero eyebrow="Code deployment" heading="Ship your Puppet code" data-i18n-attr="heading,eyebrow">
  <p data-i18n>
    Deploy environments from a control repository with g10k, built in. On
    demand or straight from a push, with a record of every deploy.
  </p>
</vox-hero>

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>See what your deploys actually reach</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n>
      A successful deploy says the code arrived, not that anything is
      running it. For every repository, the console shows the
      environments it has deployed, how large they are, when it last
      deployed, and two counts side by side: the nodes classification
      sends to its environments, and the nodes actually running from
      them.
    </p>
    <p data-i18n>
      The gap between them is the useful part. Assigned but not
      reporting means a deploy that has not reached its nodes yet;
      reporting but not assigned means nodes running code nothing sends
      them to, usually an environment left behind. The console names
      those states rather than leaving you to compare numbers.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{{screenshot "code-repositories"}}

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>One control repository, or one per team</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n-html>
      Teams that keep their own control repository no longer have to share
      one. Add each as a source, and give the ones that need it a prefix
      so two repositories with a <code>production</code> branch land in
      separate environments instead of overwriting each other.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<vox-code-block language="yaml">sources:
  control:
    remote: https://git.example.com/infra/control-repo.git
    prefix: false
  team_a:
    remote: https://git.example.com/team-a/control.git
    prefix: true</vox-code-block>

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>Every deploy leaves a trail</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n>
      Who triggered it, which source and ref, which environment it wrote,
      and whether it worked. A deploy that failed keeps its error instead
      of quietly vanishing from the list.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{{screenshot "code-deploys"}}

<vox-callout variant="tip" heading="Webhooks are per source" data-i18n-attr="heading">
  <p data-i18n>
    Each repository gets its own webhook and its own secret, so a secret
    that leaks from one cannot be used to deploy another.
  </p>
</vox-callout>
{{end}}
